        <body>
            <div xml:id="d57-a1">

                <pb facs="0177" n="61" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg61"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure—Extract.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chinese Secretary, continuing the
                        Memorandum of May 30, 1914.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-06-23">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 23, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The following table records the principal Presidential Mandates, legislative and
                    otherwise, from May 26, 1914, to June 22, 1914: <list>
                        <item>On May 26, 1914, a mandate abolishing the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Administrative Council</hi> (Cheng Chih Hui I).</item>
                        <item>The same mandate appoints Vice President Li Yuan-hung President and
                            Wang Ta-hsieh Vice President of the <hi rendition="#italic">Advisory
                                Chamber</hi> (Ts’an Cheng Yuan).</item>
                        <item>On May 28, 1914, a mandate authorizing the connecting of the <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Peking-Kalgan and Peking-Mukden Railways</hi> by
                            a line around Peking.</item>
                        <item>On June 2, 1914, a mandate defining the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Tao</hi>, or <hi rendition="#italic">Circuits</hi>, in the
                            Provinces.</item>
                        <item>On June 5, 1914, a mandate fixing the scale of punishments for <hi
                                rendition="#italic">venal offenses</hi> among officials.</item>
                        <item>On June 8, 1914, a mandate relating to the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >procedure of the Administrative Court</hi> (p’ing Cheng
                            Yuan).</item>
                        <item>On June 11, 1914, a mandate prescribing the duties and powers of the
                                <hi rendition="#italic">Provincial Bureaus of Finance</hi>.</item>
                        <item>On June 11, 1914, a mandate fixing the powers and duties of the <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Higher Provincial Courts</hi> (Kao Teng Shen
                            p’an T’ing).</item>
                        <item>On June 13, 1914, a mandate directing the Ministry of the Interior and
                            the Revenue Council to draft laws prohibiting the <hi
                                rendition="#italic">export of antiquities</hi>.</item>
                        <item>On June 15, 1914, a mandate specially appointing the <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Governors of the Provinces to supervise the
                                financial affairs of their Provinces</hi>.</item>
                        <item>On June 16, 1914, a mandate promulgating the regulations of the <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Auditor General’s Office</hi>.</item>
                        <item>On June 18, 1914, a mandate directing the Ministry of the Interior to
                            draft laws relating to <hi rendition="#italic">local
                                self-government</hi>.</item>
                        <item>[Here follows comment on the foregoing subjects.]</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
